// === PREAMBLE ===
// @trope    Throwing Down the Gauntlet
// @category Combat / Challenges
// @source   https://allthetropes.org/wiki/Throwing_Down_the_Gauntlet
// @domain   Narrative
// @scale    scene
// @version  1.3
//
// Laconic (per the wiki): not merely fighting but DEMANDING a fight — the confident, defiant act of
// challenging someone to battle, named for the medieval practice of casting down one's gauntlet. FRONTIER:
// an ACTION loaded by chivalric tradition — the formal challenge. The discriminator is a [&ThrowsGauntlet]
// event (agent=$challenger, target=$foe): a public, deliberate demand to fight. Distinct from a Badass
// Boast (self-praise) and an ambush (no declaration) — this is the open, ritual call-out that puts the
// challenge on the record.


// === IMPORTS & ASSOCIATIONS ===
import "trl/concepts/index.trl"

assoc throwing_down_the_gauntlet -> [
  the_duel     : "the challenge that opens a",
  badass_boast : "often delivered with a",
  worthy_opponent : "names a"
]


// === ONTOLOGY ===
// — founded here (the formal, defiant challenge is the discriminator; promotion model) —
verb ThrowsGauntlet(target) [+Interpersonal]   // openly, defiantly demands that another fight

imply ThrowingDownTheGauntlet -> [Opposition, Narrative]


// === ABSTRACT RULE ===
// Naming rule (scene scale): a challenger THROWS DOWN THE GAUNTLET at a foe — the challenged bound on the
// target role of a [&ThrowsGauntlet] event (agent=$challenger), so the recall keys on the agent and the
// challenge binds its foe (STYLE §8). One beat: the open, deliberate demand to fight. The dedicated
// challenge verb is the discriminator — a declared call-out, not a Badass Boast or a silent ambush.
rule Throwing_Down_The_Gauntlet {
  when:
    char $foe [+Antagonist]
    evt  $challenge [&ThrowsGauntlet(agent=$challenger, target=$foe)]
  then:
    $challenge [+ThrowingDownTheGauntlet]
}


// === CONCRETE VIGNETTE: 300 — Leonidas defies Xerxes (film, 2006) ===
// Xerxes rules the largest empire the world has seen, and demands only earth and water — submission — from
// Sparta. Leonidas answers not with a fight but with a challenge to one: he and three hundred will hold
// the Hot Gates against the god-king's millions, and he flings the defiance in Xerxes's face — "Spartans!
// Tonight we dine in Hell!" The whole campaign is a gauntlet thrown at an empire, the few openly daring
// the many.

// LEONIDAS — who openly defies the god-king
char leonidas [+Protagonist]

// XERXES — the emperor challenged to his face
char xerxes [+Antagonist]

scene the_hot_gates {
  beat 1 {
    // not submission, not an ambush — an open, defiant demand to fight
    evt the_defiance [&ThrowsGauntlet(agent=leonidas, target=xerxes)]
    // Throwing_Down_The_Gauntlet -> the_defiance [+ThrowingDownTheGauntlet]
  }
}
